From: AW1000_NSS_BYTE
Subject: [PATCH] nss-drv: stop -Werror from failing on -Wmissing-prototypes

Kernel 6.18 enables -Wmissing-prototypes for every object it compiles, and this
driver builds with -Werror, so two non-static functions in nss_core.c that have
no declaration are a hard error:

    nss_core.c:392:6: error: no previous prototype for
        'nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt' [-Werror=missing-prototypes]
    nss_core.c:2762:6: error: no previous prototype for 'nss_skb_reuse'
    cc1: all warnings being treated as errors

0121-nss-drv-add-missing-prototypes.patch declares both in nss_core.h, and that
works, but it has to be re-anchored on every nss-drv bump: the feed's own
patches move that header by several hundred lines, so a hunk written against
pristine upstream does not reach it.

Suppressing the warning from outside the driver does not work:

  - -Wno-missing-prototypes in EXTRA_CFLAGS is overridden. The driver's own
    Makefile appends `ccflags-y += -Wall -Werror`, and on the compiler command
    line the later flag wins, so -Werror restores the diagnostic.
  - ccflags-y given on the make command line (the route c15a08e took) is a
    command-line variable, which overrides every `ccflags-y += ...` in the
    driver's Makefile and loses all of the -DNSS_DRV_*_ENABLE feature defines.

So append -Wno-missing-prototypes to the same ccflags-y, immediately after the
-Wall -Werror that enables it. A -Wno-* option only disables a diagnostic and
does not change code generation, and every feature define above is untouched.

Both patches are kept: 0121 supplies the prototypes, this one makes sure a
future drift in the header cannot silently reintroduce the error.

--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -603,6 +603,7 @@
 ccflags-y += -I$(obj)/nss_data_plane/hal/include
 ccflags-y += -DNSS_PM_DEBUG_LEVEL=0 -DNSS_SKB_REUSE_SUPPORT=1
 ccflags-y += -Wall -Werror
+ccflags-y += -Wno-missing-prototypes
 
 KERNELVERSION := $(word 1, $(subst ., ,$(KERNELVERSION))).$(word 2, $(subst ., ,$(KERNELVERSION)))
 
